WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.960
A green plant builds most of its body from carbon dioxide and water.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.060
But it also needs elements from the soil.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.440
Why can't it make those?

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.240
A green plant can build all the organic molecules it needs.

5
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.140
Every amino acid, every lipid, every nucleotide, it makes for itself.

6
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.800
You cannot do that; you must eat some of yours.

7
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.910
But no plant can make an element.

8
00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.130
Each element in its body has to come in from outside.

9
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.710
That is the one thing it cannot do for itself.

10
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:42.953
Seventeen elements are conventionally essential.

11
00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:48.733
Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen come from carbon dioxide and water.

12
00:00:50.053 --> 00:00:52.273
The other fourteen are mineral nutrients.

13
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:55.653
Most plants take them chiefly from the soil.

14
00:00:56.717 --> 00:00:59.797
How the plant gets each one depends on three things.

15
00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:02.637
The form the element is in.

16
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:05.037
How far that form moves.

17
00:01:06.137 --> 00:01:07.497
And who can take it up.

18
00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:10.097
This film follows how far.

19
00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:12.397
Start with phosphate.

20
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:18.057
Here is a young root, a little behind its growing tip, in soil dotted with

21
00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:19.057
phosphate.

22
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.650
As the root takes phosphate in, the soil right around it runs low.

23
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.130
Phosphate creeps through soil so slowly that little arrives from farther out.

24
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:35.593
Root hairs help. Each is a tube grown out from a single surface cell.

25
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:41.133
Hairs push into the spaces between soil particles, past the depleted zone.

26
00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:46.393
Many plants reach farther with a fungal partner, called a mycorrhiza.

27
00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:49.973
Its threads, called hyphae, are thinner still.

28
00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:54.353
They run far out into the soil and bring phosphate back.

29
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.227
Nitrate is a different story.

30
00:01:58.547 --> 00:02:03.287
It moves through soil far more readily, and water flowing toward the root carries it

31
00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:06.647
too. For nitrate, reach matters less.

32
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.470
Inside the plant, elements can move again.

33
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.310
The phloem, the tissue that carries sugar, can move some of them out of old leaves.

34
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.550
Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium and magnesium are among them.

35
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.633
Now suppose nitrogen runs short.

36
00:02:25.853 --> 00:02:29.153
Which leaves turn yellow first, the old ones or the young ones?

37
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:41.870
The old ones. The plant pulls nitrogen out of its oldest leaves and sends it up to

38
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.410
the growing tip. So the old leaves yellow first.

39
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.410
Iron and calcium are different.

40
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.370
Once laid down, they stay largely where they are.

41
00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.930
So a shortage of iron shows first in young leaves.

42
00:02:57.033 --> 00:03:01.153
Treat these patterns as clues to test, not a key for every species.

43
00:03:02.553 --> 00:03:04.233
Different shortages can look alike.

44
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.167
So the plant faces one question again and again.

45
00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:10.607
It cannot make an element.

46
00:03:11.887 --> 00:03:15.207
How far does each nutrient move, and how will the plant reach it?
